.TH split 1 (date) "GNU coreutils (unreleased)"
.SH NAME
split \- split a file into pieces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY split
.RI [ OPTION \~.\|.\|.\&]
.RI [ FILE
.RI [ PREFIX ]]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" Add any additional description here
.PP
Output pieces of FILE to PREFIXaa, PREFIXab, ...;
default size is 1000 lines, and default PREFIX is 'x'.
.PP
With no FILE, or when FILE is \-, read standard input.
.PP
Mandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for short options too.
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-a
\fB\-a, \-\-suffix\-length=\,N\fP
.UE
generate suffixes of length N (default 2)
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-\-additional\-suffix
\fB\-\-additional\-suffix=SUFFIX\fP
.UE
append an additional SUFFIX to file names
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-b
\fB\-b, \-\-bytes=\,SIZE\fP
.UE
put SIZE bytes per output file
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-C
\fB\-C, \-\-line\-bytes=\,SIZE\fP
.UE
put at most SIZE bytes of records per output file
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-d
\fB\-d\fP
.UE
use numeric suffixes starting at 0, not alphabetic
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-\-numeric\-suffixes
\fB\-\-numeric\-suffixes[=FROM]\fP
.UE
same as \fB\-d\fR, but allow setting the start value
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-x
\fB\-x\fP
.UE
use hex suffixes starting at 0, not alphabetic
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-\-hex\-suffixes
\fB\-\-hex\-suffixes[=FROM]\fP
.UE
same as \fB\-x\fR, but allow setting the start value
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-e
\fB\-e, \-\-elide\-empty\-files\fP
.UE
do not generate empty output files with '\-n'
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-\-filter
\fB\-\-filter=COMMAND\fP
.UE
write to shell COMMAND; file name is $FILE
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-l
\fB\-l, \-\-lines=\,NUMBER\fP
.UE
put NUMBER lines/records per output file
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-n
\fB\-n, \-\-number=\,CHUNKS\fP
.UE
generate CHUNKS output files; see explanation below
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-t
\fB\-t, \-\-separator=\,SEP\fP
.UE
use SEP instead of newline as the record separator;
\&'\e0' (zero) specifies the NUL character
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-u
\fB\-u, \-\-unbuffered\fP
.UE
immediately copy input to output with '\-n r/...'
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#split\-\-verbose
\fB\-\-verbose\fP
.UE
print a diagnostic just before each output file is opened
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/split#split\-\-help
\fB\-\-help\fP
.UE
display this help and exit
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/split#split\-\-version
\fB\-\-version\fP
.UE
output version information and exit
.PP
The SIZE argument is an integer and optional unit (example: 10K is 10*1024).
Units are K,M,G,T,P,E,Z,Y,R,Q (powers of 1024) or KB,MB,... (powers of 1000).
Binary prefixes can be used, too: KiB=K, MiB=M, and so on.
.SS "CHUNKS may be:"
.TP
N
split into N files based on size of input
.TP
K/N
output Kth of N to standard output
.TP
l/N
split into N files without splitting lines/records
.TP
l/K/N
output Kth of N to standard output without splitting lines/records
.TP
r/N
like 'l' but use round robin distribution
.TP
r/K/N
likewise but only output Kth of N to standard output
.PP
\fB\-n\fR (except \fB\-nr\fR) will buffer to $TMPDIR, defaulting to \fI\,/tmp\/\fP,
if the input size cannot easily be determined.
